    {'title': 'Seaborn Assessment', 'category': 'Seaborn', 'domain': 'Data Science & AI', 'difficulty': 'Beginner', 'description': 'Statistical data visualization, themes, distribution plots, categorical plots, and heatmaps.', 'time_limit_minutes': 15, 'passing_score': 70, 'icon': 'fas fa-chart-gantt', 'badge_icon': 'fas fa-award', 'questions': [{'text': 'What is the primary advantage of Seaborn over raw Matplotlib?', 'options': ['It runs faster on mobile.', 'It provides high-level statistical plotting functions tightly integrated with Pandas DataFrames and elegant default visual themes.', 'It replaces Python with R.', 'It exports to 3D models.'], 'correct': 1, 'explanation': 'Seaborn simplifies statistical data visualization directly from structured Pandas DataFrames.'}, {'text': 'Which Seaborn function is optimal for visualizing correlation matrices with color-coded cells and numeric annotations?', 'options': ['`sns.lineplot()`', '`sns.heatmap(df.corr(), annot=True)`', '`sns.barplot()`', '`sns.scatterplot()`'], 'correct': 1, 'explanation': '`sns.heatmap()` maps matrix values to color gradients with optional cell annotations.'}, {'text': "What does `sns.boxplot(x='category', y='value', data=df)` display?", 'options': ['A 3D scatter plot.', "Side-by-side box-and-whisker plots showing quartile distributions and outliers for 'value' across each 'category' group.", 'A single bar chart.', 'A pie chart.'], 'correct': 1, 'explanation': 'Categorical boxplots compare feature distributions across discrete grouping categories.'}, {'text': 'What Seaborn function plots a univariate distribution along with a smoothed Kernel Density Estimate (KDE) curve?', 'options': ['`sns.displot()` or `sns.histplot(kde=True)`', '`sns.lineplot()`', '`sns.swarmplot()`', '`sns.stripplot()`'], 'correct': 0, 'explanation': '`histplot` with `kde=True` visualizes binned histograms overlaid with continuous probability densities.'}, {'text': 'How do you apply a dark aesthetic theme across all subsequent Seaborn plots?', 'options': ["`sns.set_theme(style='darkgrid')`", '`plt.dark()`', "`sns.color_mode('black')`", '`sns.style_dark()`'], 'correct': 0, 'explanation': '`sns.set_theme()` configures global visual styling and palette aesthetics across plots.'}]},
    {'title': 'Feature Engineering AI Assessment', 'category': 'Feature Engineering', 'domain': 'Data Science & AI', 'difficulty': 'Intermediate', 'description': 'One-hot encoding, scaling, binning, interaction terms, polynomial features, and PCA.', 'time_limit_minutes': 15, 'passing_score': 70, 'icon': 'fas fa-sliders', 'badge_icon': 'fas fa-award', 'questions': [{'text': 'What is One-Hot Encoding used for in machine learning preprocessing?', 'options': ['Normalizing continuous numbers.', 'Converting nominal categorical variables into binary (0/1) indicator columns without imposing an artificial ordinal rank.', 'Deleting duplicate rows.', 'Compressing images.'], 'correct': 1, 'explanation': 'One-hot encoding represents categorical states as orthogonal binary vectors.'}, {'text': 'What is the difference between Min-Max Scaling (Normalization) and Standard Scaling (Standardization)?', 'options': ['Normalization bounds data strictly between [0, 1]; Standardization centers data to mean=0 and variance=1 (standard deviation).', 'Standardization bounds data between [0, 1].', 'Normalization only works on negative numbers.', 'There is no difference.'], 'correct': 0, 'explanation': 'Min-Max scales to fixed ranges [0, 1]; Standardization scales to zero-mean unit-variance.'}, {'text': 'What is Principal Component Analysis (PCA) used for in feature engineering?', 'options': ['Classifying images.', 'Linear dimensionality reduction that transforms correlated features into a smaller set of orthogonal, uncorrelated principal components that maximize variance.', 'Imputing missing data.', 'Formatting text.'], 'correct': 1, 'explanation': 'PCA projects high-dimensional data onto orthogonal axes of maximal variance.'}, {'text': 'What is Target Encoding (Mean Encoding) for high-cardinality categorical features?', 'options': ['Replacing categories with their frequency.', 'Replacing each categorical value with the average target label value for that category across the training data.', 'One-hot encoding all categories.', 'Encrypting category names.'], 'correct': 1, 'explanation': 'Target encoding leverages conditional target means, reducing high-cardinality dimensionality.'}, {'text': 'What is an Interaction Feature in tabular modeling?', 'options': ['A chat log between users.', 'A new feature created by multiplying, dividing, or combining two or more existing features (e.g. `Price * Quantity`) to capture joint effects.', 'A foreign key.', 'An outlier point.'], 'correct': 1, 'explanation': 'Interaction terms model multiplicative non-linear dependencies between pairs of features.'}]},
    {'title': 'Model Evaluation AI Assessment', 'category': 'Model Evaluation', 'domain': 'Data Science & AI', 'difficulty': 'Intermediate', 'description': 'Confusion matrix, ROC-AUC, precision, recall, F1, cross-validation, and regression metrics (RMSE/MAE).', 'time_limit_minutes': 15, 'passing_score': 70, 'icon': 'fas fa-clipboard-check', 'badge_icon': 'fas fa-award', 'questions': [{'text': 'In binary classification evaluation, what is Precision?', 'options': ['True Positives / (True Positives + False Negatives)', 'True Positives / (True Positives + False Positives) (the proportion of predicted positive cases that were truly positive)', 'Total Correct / Total Samples', 'False Positives / All Negatives'], 'correct': 1, 'explanation': 'Precision measures prediction exactness: of all predicted positives, how many were correct.'}, {'text': 'What is Recall (Sensitivity)?', 'options': ['True Positives / (True Positives + False Positives)', 'True Positives / (True Positives + False Negatives) (the proportion of actual positive cases successfully detected by the model)', 'Accuracy divided by 2', 'True Negatives / All Positives'], 'correct': 1, 'explanation': 'Recall measures model completeness: of all true positive instances, how many were captured.'}, {'text': 'What does the Area Under the ROC Curve (ROC-AUC) measure?', 'options': ['The training duration of the model.', "The model's ability to discriminate between positive and negative classes across all possible classification probability thresholds (1.0 = perfect, 0.5 = random guess).", 'The number of decision trees.', 'The memory usage in MB.'], 'correct': 1, 'explanation': 'ROC-AUC evaluates ranking discrimination invariant of arbitrary classification thresholds.'}, {'text': 'What is the difference between Mean Absolute Error (MAE) and Root Mean Squared Error (RMSE) in regression?', 'options': ['MAE penalizes large outlier errors more heavily than RMSE.', 'RMSE squares error residuals before averaging and taking square root, penalizing large outlier errors more severely than linear MAE.', 'MAE is for classification only.', 'There is no difference.'], 'correct': 1, 'explanation': "RMSE's quadratic penalty makes it sensitive to large outlier deviations."}, {'text': 'What is Data Leakage in machine learning evaluation?', 'options': ['A hacker stealing the dataset.', 'When information from outside the training dataset (such as test set distributions or future temporal data) improperly influences model training, producing overly optimistic validation scores that fail in production.', 'A memory leak in Python.', 'Dropping missing values.'], 'correct': 1, 'explanation': 'Data leakage corrupts model evaluation by exposing target or test signals during training.'}]},
    {'title': 'Time Series Analysis AI Assessment', 'category': 'Time Series Analysis', 'domain': 'Data Science & AI', 'difficulty': 'Advanced', 'description': 'Stationarity, ARIMA/SARIMA, autocorrelation (ACF/PACF), seasonal decomposition, and forecasting.', 'time_limit_minutes': 20, 'passing_score': 70, 'icon': 'fas fa-timeline', 'badge_icon': 'fas fa-award', 'questions': [{'text': 'What does Stationarity mean in time series analysis?', 'options': ['The time series has no data points.', 'The statistical properties of the series (mean, variance, and autocorrelation) remain constant over time, lacking trend or seasonality.', 'The series only moves upwards.', 'The series is recorded every hour.'], 'correct': 1, 'explanation': 'Stationary series exhibit constant mean and variance over time, a prerequisite for ARIMA models.'}, {'text': 'Which statistical test is standard for testing the presence of a unit root (non-stationarity) in a time series?', 'options': ["Student's t-test", 'Augmented Dickey-Fuller (ADF) Test', 'Chi-Square Test', 'One-Way ANOVA'], 'correct': 1, 'explanation': 'The ADF test checks for unit roots: p < 0.05 indicates stationarity.'}, {'text': 'What are the three components of an ARIMA(p, d, q) forecasting model?', 'options': ['p = autoregressive terms, d = degree of differencing for stationarity, q = moving average terms.', 'p = polynomial, d = data, q = query.', 'p = price, d = demand, q = quantity.', 'p = percent, d = date, q = quartile.'], 'correct': 0, 'explanation': 'ARIMA combines Autoregression (p), Integration/Differencing (d), and Moving Averages (q).'}, {'text': 'What does the Autocorrelation Function (ACF) plot display?', 'options': ['Stock market tickers.', 'The correlation of a time series with lagged copies of itself over sequential time lags.', 'The moving average error.', 'Future predictions.'], 'correct': 1, 'explanation': 'ACF measures linear correlation between observations separated by k time intervals.'}, {'text': 'What does Seasonal Decomposition of time series split a signal into?', 'options': ['Trend, Seasonality, and Residual (Noise) components.', 'Past, Present, and Future.', 'Positive, Negative, and Zero values.', 'Weekly, Monthly, and Annual tables.'], 'correct': 0, 'explanation': 'Decomposition isolates underlying secular trends, cyclical seasonality, and stochastic noise.'}]},
    {'title': 'AWS Assessment', 'category': 'AWS', 'domain': 'Cloud & DevOps', 'difficulty': 'Intermediate', 'description': 'Amazon Web Services core services (EC2, S3, IAM, Lambda, VPC, RDS), architecture, and security.', 'time_limit_minutes': 15, 'passing_score': 70, 'icon': 'fab fa-aws', 'badge_icon': 'fas fa-award', 'questions': [{'text': 'What AWS service provides resizable on-demand virtual compute servers in the cloud?', 'options': ['Amazon S3', 'Amazon EC2 (Elastic Compute Cloud)', 'AWS Lambda', 'Amazon DynamoDB'], 'correct': 1, 'explanation': 'EC2 delivers scalable virtual machine compute instances in AWS.'}, {'text': 'What is the principle of least privilege in AWS IAM (Identity and Access Management)?', 'options': ['Giving all employees AdministratorAccess.', 'Granting users and service roles only the minimum explicit IAM permissions required to perform their specific job tasks.', 'Using root account for daily tasks.', 'Disabling Multi-Factor Authentication.'], 'correct': 1, 'explanation': 'IAM policies restrict permissions strictly to required resource operations.'}, {'text': 'What AWS serverless compute service executes code in response to events without provisioning or managing servers?', 'options': ['Amazon EC2', 'AWS Lambda', 'Amazon ECS', 'AWS Elastic Beanstalk'], 'correct': 1, 'explanation': 'Lambda runs code on-demand in response to event triggers, scaling automatically.'}, {'text': 'What AWS storage class is optimized for long-term data archiving with retrieval times ranging from minutes to hours at ultra-low cost?', 'options': ['Amazon S3 Standard', 'Amazon S3 Glacier / Glacier Flexible Archive', 'Amazon EBS', 'Amazon EFS'], 'correct': 1, 'explanation': 'S3 Glacier provides low-cost archival storage for cold data.'}, {'text': 'What does an Amazon VPC (Virtual Private Cloud) provide?', 'options': ['A domain name registrar.', 'A logically isolated virtual network dedicated to your AWS account with customizable IP ranges, subnets, route tables, and gateways.', 'A load balancer.', 'An email server.'], 'correct': 1, 'explanation': 'VPC provides isolated private software-defined networking in AWS.'}]},
    {'title': 'Microsoft Azure Assessment', 'category': 'Microsoft Azure', 'domain': 'Cloud & DevOps', 'difficulty': 'Intermediate', 'description': 'Azure Virtual Machines, Blob Storage, Azure Functions, Entra ID (Azure AD), and Virtual Networks.', 'time_limit_minutes': 15, 'passing_score': 70, 'icon': 'fab fa-microsoft', 'badge_icon': 'fas fa-award', 'questions': [{'text': 'What is Microsoft Entra ID (formerly Azure Active Directory)?', 'options': ['A database table.', "Microsoft's cloud-based identity and access management service for user authentication, SSO, and RBAC across cloud apps.", 'A virtual machine operating system.', 'An antivirus software.'], 'correct': 1, 'explanation': 'Entra ID manages enterprise identity, single sign-on, and conditional access policies.'}, {'text': 'What Azure compute resource provides serverless event-driven execution similar to AWS Lambda?', 'options': ['Azure Virtual Machine', 'Azure Functions', 'Azure Kubernetes Service', 'Azure App Service'], 'correct': 1, 'explanation': 'Azure Functions executes serverless event-driven code blocks on demand.'}, {'text': 'What Azure storage service is designed for storing massive amounts of unstructured object data (images, videos, backups)?', 'options': ['Azure SQL Database', 'Azure Blob Storage', 'Azure Cosmos DB', 'Azure Table Storage'], 'correct': 1, 'explanation': "Blob Storage is Azure's scalable object store for unstructured text and binary files."}, {'text': 'What is an Azure Resource Group?', 'options': ['A server cluster.', 'A logical container that holds and groups related Azure resources for unified deployment, management, and billing lifecycle.', 'A network firewall.', 'A user group in Windows.'], 'correct': 1, 'explanation': 'Resource groups organize and govern associated cloud resources collectively.'}, {'text': 'What is Azure Resource Manager (ARM) / Bicep used for?', 'options': ['Designing logos.', 'Declarative Infrastructure as Code (IaC) templates for deploying and managing Azure infrastructure consistently.', 'Auditing employee emails.', 'Running machine learning models.'], 'correct': 1, 'explanation': 'ARM templates and Bicep provide declarative IaC orchestration in Azure.'}]},
    {'title': 'Google Cloud Assessment', 'category': 'Google Cloud', 'domain': 'Cloud & DevOps', 'difficulty': 'Intermediate', 'description': 'GCP compute (Compute Engine, Cloud Run, GKE), BigQuery, Cloud Storage, and IAM.', 'time_limit_minutes': 15, 'passing_score': 70, 'icon': 'fab fa-google', 'badge_icon': 'fas fa-award', 'questions': [{'text': 'What Google Cloud service provides fully managed, serverless execution of containerized applications via HTTP requests?', 'options': ['Compute Engine', 'Cloud Run', 'Google Kubernetes Engine', 'App Engine Standard'], 'correct': 1, 'explanation': 'Cloud Run runs stateless containers in a fully managed serverless environment with automatic scale-to-zero.'}, {'text': 'What is Google Cloud BigQuery?', 'options': ['A NoSQL document database.', 'A fully managed, petabyte-scale serverless cloud data warehouse with built-in SQL analytics and machine learning.', 'A virtual machine cluster.', 'A file storage bucket.'], 'correct': 1, 'explanation': 'BigQuery delivers fast SQL analytics across massive datasets with serverless columnar architecture.'}, {'text': 'What GCP service provides managed enterprise container orchestration powered by open-source Kubernetes?', 'options': ['Google Kubernetes Engine (GKE)', 'Compute Engine', 'Cloud Functions', 'Cloud Spanner'], 'correct': 0, 'explanation': "GKE is Google's managed environment for deploying and managing containerized applications at scale."}, {'text': 'What is Google Cloud Storage (GCS)?', 'options': ['A relational database.', 'A scalable object storage service for storing and serving unstructured files with global edge availability.', 'A virtual hard drive for Linux only.', 'A code repository.'], 'correct': 1, 'explanation': 'GCS provides secure, durable object storage with multi-region redundancy.'}, {'text': 'What are Service Accounts in Google Cloud Platform IAM?', 'options': ['Personal Google email accounts.', 'Special identity accounts used by applications and virtual workloads to authenticate and make authorized GCP API calls securely.', 'Billing accounts.', 'Support ticket accounts.'], 'correct': 1, 'explanation': 'Service accounts authenticate machines and services without human credentials.'}]},
    {'title': 'Docker Assessment', 'category': 'Docker', 'domain': 'Cloud & DevOps', 'difficulty': 'Intermediate', 'description': 'Containerization, Dockerfiles, multi-stage builds, images, volumes, and Docker Compose.', 'time_limit_minutes': 15, 'passing_score': 70, 'icon': 'fab fa-docker', 'badge_icon': 'fas fa-award', 'questions': [{'text': 'What is the fundamental difference between a Docker Container and a traditional Virtual Machine (VM)?', 'options': ['Containers virtualize the underlying OS kernel and share it across isolated user-space processes, whereas VMs virtualize full hardware and run complete guest operating systems.', 'Containers require more RAM than VMs.', 'VMs cannot run Linux.', 'There is no difference.'], 'correct': 0, 'explanation': 'Containers are lightweight processes sharing the host OS kernel; VMs emulate full hardware.'}, {'text': 'What is a Docker Multi-Stage Build and why is it used?', 'options': ['Building multiple containers at once.', 'Using multiple `FROM` instructions in a single Dockerfile to separate compilation tools from the lean final runtime image, drastically reducing production image sizes.', 'Running Docker on two servers.', 'Compressing images with zip.'], 'correct': 1, 'explanation': 'Multi-stage builds leave compiler toolchains behind, producing minimal secure runtime images.'}, {'text': 'What Docker mechanism is used to persist data generated by containers on the host filesystem across container restarts and deletions?', 'options': ['Docker Volumes / Bind Mounts', 'Environment variables', 'Docker Tags', 'Docker Layers'], 'correct': 0, 'explanation': 'Volumes store persistent state independently from container lifecycles on the host.'}, {'text': 'What is Docker Compose?', 'options': ['A compiler for C++.', 'A tool for defining and running multi-container Docker applications using a declarative YAML configuration file.', 'A graphical Docker desktop app.', 'A network cable.'], 'correct': 1, 'explanation': 'Docker Compose orchestrates multi-service container stacks (e.g. web + db + redis) declaratively.'}, {'text': 'What does the `.dockerignore` file do?', 'options': ['Deletes docker images.', 'Specifies file and directory patterns that should be excluded from the build context when building Docker images, speeding up builds and preventing secret leakage.', 'Disables container networking.', 'Stops running containers.'], 'correct': 1, 'explanation': '.dockerignore prevents local files (like .env or node_modules) from uploading into build contexts.'}]},
    {'title': 'Kubernetes Assessment', 'category': 'Kubernetes', 'domain': 'Cloud & DevOps', 'difficulty': 'Advanced', 'description': 'K8s architecture, Pods, Deployments, Services, ConfigMaps/Secrets, and Ingress controllers.', 'time_limit_minutes': 20, 'passing_score': 70, 'icon': 'fas fa-dharmachakra', 'badge_icon': 'fas fa-award', 'questions': [{'text': 'What is the smallest deployable atomic compute unit in Kubernetes?', 'options': ['Node', 'Pod (containing one or more tightly coupled containers)', 'Container', 'Deployment'], 'correct': 1, 'explanation': 'Pods encapsulate one or more containers sharing network namespaces and storage volumes.'}, {'text': 'What is the role of the Kubernetes Control Plane component `kube-scheduler`?', 'options': ['Stores cluster state.', 'Assigns newly created Pods to appropriate Worker Nodes based on resource constraints, affinity, and availability.', 'Monitors container logs.', 'Controls DNS routing.'], 'correct': 1, 'explanation': 'The scheduler determines optimal node placement for unscheduled pods.'}, {'text': 'What Kubernetes resource ensures that a specified number of identical Pod replicas are running and handles zero-downtime rolling updates?', 'options': ['StatefulSet', 'Deployment (and ReplicaSet)', 'DaemonSet', 'Job'], 'correct': 1, 'explanation': 'Deployments manage ReplicaSets to orchestrate declarative rolling updates and replica counts.'}, {'text': 'What Kubernetes Service type exposes a set of Pods outside the cluster by provisioning an external cloud provider load balancer?', 'options': ['ClusterIP', 'NodePort', 'LoadBalancer', 'ExternalName'], 'correct': 2, 'explanation': 'Service type LoadBalancer provisions an external cloud load balancer targeting cluster nodes.'}, {'text': 'What is a Kubernetes Ingress Controller?', 'options': ['A database replica.', 'An intelligent reverse proxy (e.g. NGINX, Traefik) that manages external HTTP/HTTPS traffic routing to internal cluster services based on path/host rules.', 'A cluster backup tool.', 'A node monitor.'], 'correct': 1, 'explanation': 'Ingress routes inbound HTTP requests to internal Services based on hostname and URI paths.'}]},
    {'title': 'Git Assessment', 'category': 'Git', 'domain': 'Cloud & DevOps', 'difficulty': 'Beginner', 'description': 'Version control fundamentals, commits, branching, merging, rebasing, and resolving merge conflicts.', 'time_limit_minutes': 15, 'passing_score': 70, 'icon': 'fab fa-git-alt', 'badge_icon': 'fas fa-award', 'questions': [{'text': 'In Git, what is the staging area (Index)?', 'options': ['The remote server repository.', 'An intermediate holding area where changes are formatted and reviewed using `git add` before committing them to history.', 'The trash bin.', 'A temporary branch.'], 'correct': 1, 'explanation': 'The staging area buffers changes curated for the next atomic snapshot commit.'}, {'text': 'What is the difference between `git merge` and `git rebase`?', 'options': ['`git merge` creates a new merge commit combining two histories; `git rebase` rewrites commit history by replaying commits on top of the target base branch linearly.', '`git rebase` deletes files.', '`git merge` is for remote branches only.', 'There is no difference.'], 'correct': 0, 'explanation': 'Merge preserves original commit chronology with a join commit; rebase linearizes history.'}, {'text': 'What command undoes uncommitted modifications in a tracked file in modern Git (2.23+)?', 'options': ['`git restore <file>`', '`git cancel <file>`', '`git drop <file>`', '`git erase <file>`'], 'correct': 0, 'explanation': '`git restore` discards unstaged working tree changes back to HEAD state.'}, {'text': 'What does `git cherry-pick <commit-hash>` accomplish?', 'options': ['Deletes the commit from history.', 'Applies the exact changes introduced by a specific existing commit from another branch onto the current active branch.', 'Creates a pull request.', 'Merges all branches.'], 'correct': 1, 'explanation': 'Cherry-picking applies an isolated individual commit onto the active HEAD branch.'}, {'text': 'What file specifies intentionally untracked files and folders that Git should ignore (e.g. `.env`, `node_modules`)?', 'options': ['.gitconfig', '.gitignore', '.gitmodules', '.gitattributes'], 'correct': 1, 'explanation': '`.gitignore` excludes temporary artifacts, dependencies, and credentials from version tracking.'}]},
    {'title': 'GitHub Assessment', 'category': 'GitHub', 'domain': 'Cloud & DevOps', 'difficulty': 'Beginner', 'description': 'Pull requests, code review workflows, GitHub Actions CI, branch protection, and issues.', 'time_limit_minutes': 15, 'passing_score': 70, 'icon': 'fab fa-github', 'badge_icon': 'fas fa-award', 'questions': [{'text': 'What is a Pull Request (PR) in GitHub collaborative workflows?', 'options': ['A command to download files.', 'A proposed set of code changes submitted from a feature branch to a target branch for automated testing, peer review, and discussion before merging.', 'A bug report.', 'A server reboot request.'], 'correct': 1, 'explanation': 'Pull Requests facilitate code reviews, CI checks, and discussion before merging branches.'}, {'text': 'What is GitHub Actions used for?', 'options': ['Writing documentation.', 'Automating continuous integration (CI) and continuous deployment (CD) workflows triggered by GitHub repository events (push, PR, release).', 'Buying cloud domains.', 'Hosting video chats.'], 'correct': 1, 'explanation': 'GitHub Actions executes automated CI/CD pipelines defined in `.github/workflows/*.yml`.'}, {'text': "What do Branch Protection Rules enforce on a repository's `main` branch?", 'options': ['They prevent the branch from being read.', 'They require passing CI status checks, mandatory peer review approvals, and prevent force pushes before code can be merged.', 'They delete old branches.', 'They lock the repository permanently.'], 'correct': 1, 'explanation': 'Branch protection guards production code by requiring PR reviews and clean test pipelines.'}, {'text': 'What is a GitHub Fork?', 'options': ['A command-line tool.', "A personal remote copy of another user's repository on your GitHub account, allowing independent modifications without affecting the upstream original.", 'A branch deletion.', 'A merge conflict.'], 'correct': 1, 'explanation': 'Forks create independent personal copies of upstream open-source repositories.'}, {'text': 'What does the GitHub `CODEOWNERS` file specify?', 'options': ['Who pays the GitHub bill.', 'The specific individuals or teams automatically requested for code reviews when a PR modifies matching file path patterns.', 'The open-source license.', "The author's contact information."], 'correct': 1, 'explanation': 'CODEOWNERS automates mandatory reviewer assignments based on modified repository paths.'}]},
    {'title': 'CI/CD Assessment', 'category': 'CI/CD', 'domain': 'Cloud & DevOps', 'difficulty': 'Intermediate', 'description': 'Continuous Integration, Continuous Deployment, automated test pipelines, and deployment strategies.', 'time_limit_minutes': 15, 'passing_score': 70, 'icon': 'fas fa-arrows-split-up-and-left', 'badge_icon': 'fas fa-award', 'questions': [{'text': 'What is the primary objective of Continuous Integration (CI)?', 'options': ['To deploy to production once a year.', 'To automatically integrate code changes from multiple contributors into a shared mainline repository frequently, validating each integration with automated builds and test suites.', 'To replace human testers with bots entirely.', 'To compress source code.'], 'correct': 1, 'explanation': 'CI catches integration regressions early through automated test runs on every pull request.'}, {'text': 'What is the difference between Continuous Delivery and Continuous Deployment?', 'options': ['Continuous Delivery automatically prepares tested build artifacts ready for one-click manual production release; Continuous Deployment automatically deploys passing builds directly to production without human intervention.', 'Continuous Delivery is for mobile apps only.', 'Continuous Deployment requires manual sign-off.', 'There is no difference.'], 'correct': 0, 'explanation': 'Delivery stops before automated production release; Deployment releases passing builds automatically.'}, {'text': 'What is a Blue-Green Deployment strategy?', 'options': ['Deploying half the code on Linux and half on Windows.', 'Maintaining two identical production environments (Blue active, Green idle); new versions deploy to Green, and upon passing health checks, router traffic instantly switches to Green with zero downtime.', 'Deploying code on weekends only.', 'Using blue and green CSS themes.'], 'correct': 1, 'explanation': 'Blue-Green enables instant zero-downtime cutover and simple fallback by routing traffic between environments.'}, {'text': 'What is a Canary Deployment in modern DevOps?', 'options': ['Testing code on bird sanctuaries.', 'Rolling out a new software release to a small percentage of real users (e.g. 5%) to observe error rates and telemetry before gradually promoting to 100% of traffic.', 'Deleting old servers.', 'Simulating network crashes.'], 'correct': 1, 'explanation': 'Canary releases validate new versions against production traffic with minimal blast radius.'}, {'text': 'What is a Linter in automated CI pipelines?', 'options': ['A tool that compiles binaries.', 'A static code analysis tool that flags programming bugs, syntax errors, security flaws, and stylistic inconsistencies without executing the code.', 'A test runner for unit tests.', 'A database migration tool.'], 'correct': 1, 'explanation': 'Linters enforce static code quality, type safety, and formatting standards during CI.'}]},
    {'title': 'Linux Assessment', 'category': 'Linux', 'domain': 'Cloud & DevOps', 'difficulty': 'Intermediate', 'description': 'Linux filesystem hierarchy, permissions (chmod/chown), processes (ps/top/kill), signals, and systemd.', 'time_limit_minutes': 15, 'passing_score': 70, 'icon': 'fab fa-linux', 'badge_icon': 'fas fa-award', 'questions': [{'text': "In the Linux permission string `-rwxr-xr--`, what permissions does the 'group' have on the file?", 'options': ['Read, Write, Execute (7)', 'Read and Execute only (r-x = 5)', 'Read only (r-- = 4)', 'No permissions (0)'], 'correct': 1, 'explanation': 'Permissions are formatted as [user: rwx][group: r-x][others: r--].'}, {'text': 'Which Linux command displays active system processes, real-time CPU utilization, memory consumption, and system load averages?', 'options': ['`top` or `htop`', '`df -h`', '`ls -la`', '`chmod`'], 'correct': 0, 'explanation': '`top` and `htop` monitor interactive process table statistics and CPU/RAM loads.'}, {'text': 'What is the default signal sent to a process when executing `kill <PID>` without flags?', 'options': ['SIGKILL (9) - uncatchable immediate termination', 'SIGTERM (15) - polite termination signal allowing graceful cleanup', 'SIGSTOP (19)', 'SIGHUP (1)'], 'correct': 1, 'explanation': 'kill defaults to SIGTERM (signal 15), requesting polite shutdown before SIGKILL (9) is forced.'}, {'text': 'What is `systemd` in modern Linux distributions?', 'options': ['A text editor.', 'The standard system and service manager that initializes user space, manages daemons, controls startup sequencing, and logs events via `journalctl`.', 'A network firewall.', 'A package manager.'], 'correct': 1, 'explanation': 'systemd is the standard init system controlling system services (`systemctl`) on modern Linux.'}, {'text': 'What does the `sudo` command allow authorized users to do in Linux?', 'options': ['Reboot the computer only.', 'Execute commands with elevated security privileges (typically as the superuser/root) based on the `/etc/sudoers` policy file.', "Delete other users' files.", 'Format hard drives.'], 'correct': 1, 'explanation': 'sudo permits executing privileged administrative tasks safely without logging in directly as root.'}]},
    {'title': 'Bash Assessment', 'category': 'Bash', 'domain': 'Cloud & DevOps', 'difficulty': 'Intermediate', 'description': 'Shell scripting, pipelines, redirection (>/>>/2>&1), variables, loops, and regex grep/sed/awk.', 'time_limit_minutes': 15, 'passing_score': 70, 'icon': 'fas fa-terminal', 'badge_icon': 'fas fa-award', 'questions': [{'text': 'What does `2>&1` accomplish in a Bash command line (e.g. `command > output.log 2>&1`)?', 'options': ['Doubles the execution speed.', 'Redirects Standard Error (file descriptor 2) to the same stream destination as Standard Output (file descriptor 1).', 'Sends output to two printers.', 'Disables error logging.'], 'correct': 1, 'explanation': '`2>&1` merges stderr into the stdout stream for unified logging.'}, {'text': "What is the purpose of the 'shebang' line `#!/bin/bash` at the very beginning of a script file?", 'options': ['It is a comment for human developers.', 'It tells the operating system kernel which interpreter to execute the script with when run as an executable.', 'It marks the script as encrypted.', 'It deletes the file on finish.'], 'correct': 1, 'explanation': 'The shebang tells the kernel execve() syscall which binary interpreter to spawn.'}, {'text': 'What does the exit status `$?` of `0` indicate in standard Bash scripting convention?', 'options': ['The previous command failed with an unknown error.', 'The previous command executed successfully without errors.', 'The command is still running in background.', 'Memory overflow.'], 'correct': 1, 'explanation': 'Exit code 0 signals success; any non-zero integer represents a specific failure code.'}, {'text': 'Which text processing tool is specialized for pattern searching and filtering lines from text streams using regular expressions?', 'options': ['`grep`', '`tar`', '`cron`', '`mkdir`'], 'correct': 0, 'explanation': '`grep` searches files and streams for lines matching specified regular expressions.'}, {'text': 'What does the command `set -e` (or `set -o errexit`) do inside a Bash script?', 'options': ['Suppresses all console output.', 'Instructs Bash to immediately exit the script if any command exits with a non-zero (failure) status code.', 'Enables debug mode.', 'Runs the script on multiple CPU cores.'], 'correct': 1, 'explanation': '`set -e` halts script execution on errors, preventing runaway failures in pipelines.'}]},
    {'title': 'Cloud Fundamentals', 'category': 'Cloud Fundamentals', 'domain': 'Cloud & DevOps', 'difficulty': 'Beginner', 'description': 'Cloud deployment models (Public, Private, Hybrid), service models (IaaS, PaaS, SaaS), and high availability.', 'time_limit_minutes': 15, 'passing_score': 70, 'icon': 'fas fa-cloud', 'badge_icon': 'fas fa-award', 'questions': [{'text': 'What is the difference between IaaS (Infrastructure as a Service), PaaS (Platform as a Service), and SaaS (Software as a Service)?', 'options': ['IaaS provides raw compute/networking; PaaS provides application runtime environments without server management; SaaS delivers fully managed end-user applications over web.', 'PaaS is on-premise only.', 'SaaS requires writing C++ code.', 'There is no difference.'], 'correct': 0, 'explanation': 'IaaS offers virtual infrastructure; PaaS provides development platforms; SaaS delivers ready software applications.'}, {'text': 'What is the definition of High Availability (HA) in cloud architecture?', 'options': ['Having the fastest network connection.', "Designing systems to operate continuously without single points of failure, typically targeting 99.99% ('four nines') or higher uptime through redundancy.", 'Backing up files to external flash drives.', 'Using dark mode themes.'], 'correct': 1, 'explanation': 'HA eliminates single points of failure via automated multi-zone/multi-region failovers.'}, {'text': 'What is an Availability Zone (AZ) in major cloud providers (AWS/Azure/GCP)?', 'options': ['A geographical continent.', 'One or more discrete, physically separated data centers within a region, equipped with independent power, cooling, and low-latency networking.', 'A security firewall.', 'A customer support tier.'], 'correct': 1, 'explanation': 'AZs are isolated data center facilities designed for fault tolerance within a metro region.'}, {'text': 'What does Cloud Elasticity mean?', 'options': ['The ability of cloud hardware to bend physically.', 'The ability of a cloud system to automatically provision and de-provision compute/storage resources in real time to match fluctuating demand.', 'Paying a fixed monthly flat fee.', 'Using SSD storage.'], 'correct': 1, 'explanation': 'Elasticity dynamically scales infrastructure up or down based on live workload metrics.'}, {'text': 'What is the Shared Responsibility Model in cloud security?', 'options': ['The cloud provider is 100% responsible for all security.', 'The cloud provider manages security OF the cloud (infrastructure, hardware, facilities), while the customer manages security IN the cloud (data, IAM, OS patches, configurations).', 'Customers only pay for electricity.', 'Security is managed by third-party auditors.'], 'correct': 1, 'explanation': 'Shared responsibility divides operational security between cloud vendors and customer tenants.'}]},
    {'title': 'DevOps Fundamentals', 'category': 'DevOps Fundamentals', 'domain': 'Cloud & DevOps', 'difficulty': 'Beginner', 'description': 'DevOps lifecycle, cultural philosophies, CALMS framework, observability, and infrastructure as code.', 'time_limit_minutes': 15, 'passing_score': 70, 'icon': 'fas fa-infinity', 'badge_icon': 'fas fa-award', 'questions': [{'text': 'What is the primary cultural and operational objective of the DevOps methodology?', 'options': ['Replacing software developers with system administrators.', 'Breaking down organizational silos between Development and IT Operations to shorten development lifecycles, increase deployment frequency, and deliver dependable software continuously.', 'Eliminating code reviews.', 'Using Docker exclusively.'], 'correct': 1, 'explanation': 'DevOps unifies engineering and operations teams through automation, shared ownership, and continuous feedback.'}, {'text': "What does the 'CALMS' framework for DevOps stand for?", 'options': ['Culture, Automation, Lean, Measurement, Sharing', 'Code, Architecture, Linux, Monitoring, Security', 'Continuous, Agile, Local, Modern, Standard', 'Cloud, Automated, Logical, Managed, Stable'], 'correct': 0, 'explanation': 'CALMS encapsulates the five foundational pillars of successful DevOps adoption.'}, {'text': 'What is Infrastructure as Code (IaC) (e.g. Terraform, Ansible, CloudFormation)?', 'options': ['Writing code comments about servers.', 'Managing, provisioning, and versioning cloud infrastructure resources using machine-readable configuration definition files rather than manual console clicking.', 'Compiling Linux kernels.', 'Running bash scripts manually.'], 'correct': 1, 'explanation': 'IaC enables declarative, auditable, and automated infrastructure provisioning.'}, {'text': 'What are the Three Pillars of Observability in modern distributed systems?', 'options': ['CPU, RAM, and Disk Space', 'Metrics, Logs, and Distributed Traces', 'Speed, Security, and Scalability', 'Frontend, Backend, and Database'], 'correct': 1, 'explanation': 'Metrics, Logs, and Traces provide complete visibility into system state and distributed latency.'}, {'text': 'What is Mean Time to Recovery (MTTR) in DevOps metrics?', 'options': ['The time it takes to build a new feature.', 'The average time required for a team to recover and restore normal service operations following an unexpected production outage or failure.', 'The duration of daily standup meetings.', 'The time to compile code.'], 'correct': 1, 'explanation': 'MTTR measures operational resilience and incident recovery speed.'}]},
    {'title': 'Data Structures Assessment', 'category': 'Data Structures', 'domain': 'Software Engineering', 'difficulty': 'Intermediate', 'description': 'Arrays, linked lists, stacks, queues, hash tables, binary search trees, heaps, and graphs.', 'time_limit_minutes': 15, 'passing_score': 70, 'icon': 'fas fa-diagram-nested', 'badge_icon': 'fas fa-award', 'questions': [{'text': 'What is the average time complexity of searching for an element in a balanced Binary Search Tree (e.g. AVL, Red-Black Tree)?', 'options': ['O(1)', 'O(log n)', 'O(n)', 'O(n log n)'], 'correct': 1, 'explanation': 'Balanced BSTs halve search space at each branch level, guaranteeing O(log n) lookups.'}, {'text': 'Which data structure operates on a Last-In, First-Out (LIFO) order of element removal?', 'options': ['Queue', 'Stack', 'Heap', 'Linked List'], 'correct': 1, 'explanation': 'Stacks enforce LIFO ordering, where the most recently pushed element is popped first.'}, {'text': 'What is a Max-Heap data structure?', 'options': ['A sorted array.', 'A complete binary tree where the value of every parent node is greater than or equal to the values of its children, keeping the maximum element at the root.', 'A hash table with chaining.', 'A graph without cycles.'], 'correct': 1, 'explanation': 'Max-Heaps ensure constant-time O(1) access to the maximum key.'}, {'text': 'What is the worst-case time complexity of inserting a node at the head of a Singly Linked List when given the head pointer?', 'options': ['O(1)', 'O(n)', 'O(log n)', 'O(n^2)'], 'correct': 0, 'explanation': "Prepending to the head requires only updating the new node's next pointer and head reference in O(1) constant time."}, {'text': 'What technique is used to resolve hash collisions in a Hash Table by storing multiple collided entries in linked lists at each bucket index?', 'options': ['Open Addressing', 'Separate Chaining', 'Binary Search', 'Tree Balancing'], 'correct': 1, 'explanation': 'Separate chaining maintains a linked chain of collided key-value pairs at each hash slot.'}]},
    {'title': 'Algorithms Assessment', 'category': 'Algorithms', 'domain': 'Software Engineering', 'difficulty': 'Advanced', 'description': 'Sorting (QuickSort, MergeSort), dynamic programming, greedy methods, Dijkstra, and Big-O notation.', 'time_limit_minutes': 20, 'passing_score': 70, 'icon': 'fas fa-code-fork', 'badge_icon': 'fas fa-award', 'questions': [{'text': 'What is the worst-case time complexity of Merge Sort, and does it maintain stability?', 'options': ['O(n^2), unstable', 'O(n log n), stable (preserves relative order of duplicate elements)', 'O(n), stable', 'O(log n), unstable'], 'correct': 1, 'explanation': 'Merge Sort guarantees O(n log n) across all cases and is stable.'}, {'text': 'What algorithmic paradigm solves complex problems by breaking them down into overlapping subproblems and storing subproblem solutions in a lookup table (memoization/tabulation)?', 'options': ['Greedy Approach', 'Dynamic Programming', 'Brute Force', 'Divide and Conquer'], 'correct': 1, 'explanation': 'Dynamic Programming optimizes overlapping recursive subproblems via memoization or tabulation.'}, {'text': 'What is the primary difference between Breadth-First Search (BFS) and Depth-First Search (DFS) for graph traversal?', 'options': ['BFS uses a Stack; DFS uses a Queue.', 'BFS traverses level-by-level using a Queue (ideal for shortest paths on unweighted graphs); DFS explores as deep as possible along each branch using a Stack/Recursion.', 'DFS only works on trees.', 'There is no difference.'], 'correct': 1, 'explanation': 'BFS explores radial neighborhood rings; DFS probes deep along individual branches.'}, {'text': "What does Dijkstra's Algorithm compute on a weighted graph with non-negative edge weights?", 'options': ['Minimum Spanning Tree.', 'The shortest paths from a single source node to all other nodes in the graph.', 'The maximum flow.', 'Topological sorting.'], 'correct': 1, 'explanation': 'Dijkstra finds single-source shortest paths using a priority queue on non-negative weighted graphs.'}, {'text': 'What is the Big-O time complexity of binary search on a sorted array of n elements?', 'options': ['O(1)', 'O(log n)', 'O(n)', 'O(n log n)'], 'correct': 1, 'explanation': 'Binary search divides the candidate search window in half on each comparison step in O(log n) time.'}]},
    {'title': 'Object-Oriented Programming Assessment', 'category': 'Object-Oriented Programming', 'domain': 'Software Engineering', 'difficulty': 'Intermediate', 'description': 'Encapsulation, abstraction, inheritance, polymorphism, and SOLID principles.', 'time_limit_minutes': 15, 'passing_score': 70, 'icon': 'fas fa-shapes', 'badge_icon': 'fas fa-award', 'questions': [{'text': 'What are the Four Core Pillars of Object-Oriented Programming (OOP)?', 'options': ['Functions, Loops, Conditions, Variables', 'Encapsulation, Abstraction, Inheritance, Polymorphism', 'HTML, CSS, JavaScript, SQL', 'Input, Process, Output, Storage'], 'correct': 1, 'explanation': 'Encapsulation, Abstraction, Inheritance, and Polymorphism form the foundation of OOP.'}, {'text': "What does the 'S' in the SOLID object-oriented design principles stand for?", 'options': ['Simple Architecture', 'Single Responsibility Principle (a class should have only one reason to change)', 'Security First', 'Synchronous Processing'], 'correct': 1, 'explanation': 'Single Responsibility dictates that each class encapsulates a single discrete domain concern.'}, {'text': 'What is Method Overriding (Runtime Polymorphism)?', 'options': ['Writing two methods with the same name but different parameter types in the same class.', 'A subclass providing a specific implementation for a method that is already defined in its superclass, resolved dynamically at runtime.', 'Deleting a method in a subclass.', 'Encrypting method bodies.'], 'correct': 1, 'explanation': 'Method overriding replaces base class implementations with derived class logic via late binding.'}, {'text': 'What is the Liskov Substitution Principle (LSP) in SOLID?', 'options': ['Objects of a superclass should be replaceable with objects of its subclasses without breaking application correctness.', 'All classes must inherit from Object.', 'Functions must return void.', 'Interfaces must be private.'], 'correct': 0, 'explanation': 'LSP ensures subclasses honor the contracts and behavioral expectations established by base types.'}, {'text': 'What is the difference between Composition and Inheritance?', 'options': ["Composition models a 'Has-A' relationship by holding instance references; Inheritance models an 'Is-A' hierarchy relationship.", 'Composition is deprecated.', 'Inheritance cannot reuse code.', 'There is no difference.'], 'correct': 0, 'explanation': "Composition favors flexible object assembly ('Has-A') over rigid inheritance hierarchies ('Is-A')."}]},
    {'title': 'Software Engineering', 'category': 'Software Engineering', 'domain': 'Software Engineering', 'difficulty': 'Intermediate', 'description': 'SDLC models, code review best practices, technical debt management, and architectural patterns.', 'time_limit_minutes': 15, 'passing_score': 70, 'icon': 'fas fa-laptop-code', 'badge_icon': 'fas fa-award', 'questions': [{'text': 'What is Technical Debt in software engineering?', 'options': ['The monetary cost of software licenses.', 'The implied future cost of rework and maintenance incurred by choosing an expedient, suboptimal solution now instead of a well-architected approach.', 'Money owed to cloud providers.', 'Bank loans taken by startups.'], 'correct': 1, 'explanation': 'Technical debt reflects future engineering friction caused by shortcut implementations.'}, {'text': 'What is the main difference between the Agile methodology and the traditional Waterfall model?', 'options': ['Agile develops software iteratively in short sprints with continuous feedback and adaptable requirements; Waterfall follows rigid, sequential linear phases.', 'Waterfall is faster than Agile.', 'Agile does not write code.', 'Waterfall is only for mobile apps.'], 'correct': 0, 'explanation': 'Agile emphasizes iterative delivery, collaboration, and rapid adaptation to shifting priorities.'}, {'text': 'What is the purpose of Code Reviews in software engineering teams?', 'options': ['To grade junior engineers.', 'To improve code quality, share domain knowledge, identify potential bugs and security vulnerabilities, and ensure adherence to architectural standards before merging.', 'To format indentation.', 'To delay project releases.'], 'correct': 1, 'explanation': 'Code reviews ensure collective code ownership, defect detection, and architectural consistency.'}, {'text': "What is the DRY (Don't Repeat Yourself) principle?", 'options': ['Never write unit tests.', 'Aiming to reduce repetition of software patterns and domain logic by replacing duplicate code with abstractions or data normalization.', 'Writing code in one file only.', 'Deleting backup repositories.'], 'correct': 1, 'explanation': 'DRY states that every piece of knowledge must have a single, unambiguous representation within a system.'}, {'text': 'What does Semantic Versioning (SemVer: `MAJOR.MINOR.PATCH`) specify?', 'options': ['`MAJOR` for breaking API changes, `MINOR` for backwards-compatible features, `PATCH` for backwards-compatible bug fixes.', '`MAJOR` for year, `MINOR` for month, `PATCH` for day.', '`MAJOR` for file size in MB.', 'Random version numbering.'], 'correct': 0, 'explanation': 'SemVer communicates the nature of code modifications clearly to consumers.'}]},
    {'title': 'System Design Assessment', 'category': 'System Design', 'domain': 'Software Engineering', 'difficulty': 'Advanced', 'description': 'Scalability, load balancing, CAP theorem, caching (Redis), partitioning, and microservices.', 'time_limit_minutes': 20, 'passing_score': 70, 'icon': 'fas fa-network-wired', 'badge_icon': 'fas fa-award', 'questions': [{'text': 'What does the CAP Theorem state regarding distributed database systems?', 'options': ['A system can achieve Consistency, Availability, and Partition Tolerance simultaneously.', 'In the presence of a network partition (P), a distributed system must choose between guaranteeing Consistency (C) or Availability (A), but cannot provide both.', 'Data is always consistent across all nodes.', 'Network partitions never happen in cloud data centers.'], 'correct': 1, 'explanation': 'CAP theorem proves that during network partitions, distributed systems must trade off strong consistency against 100% availability.'}, {'text': 'What is the difference between Horizontal Scaling (Scaling Out) and Vertical Scaling (Scaling Up)?', 'options': ['Horizontal adds more machine nodes to a distributed cluster; Vertical upgrades the CPU, RAM, or storage of a single existing server machine.', 'Vertical is cheaper than Horizontal always.', 'Horizontal scaling has a hard hardware ceiling.', 'There is no difference.'], 'correct': 0, 'explanation': 'Horizontal scaling distributes load across multiple machines; Vertical scaling adds hardware to a single node.'}, {'text': 'What is the Cache-Aside (Lazy Loading) caching pattern?', 'options': ['The application queries the cache first; on a cache miss, it reads from the database, writes the fetched data into the cache for future reads, and returns the result.', 'The database updates the cache automatically on every write.', 'Data is cached in browser RAM only.', 'Cache is cleared every second.'], 'correct': 0, 'explanation': 'Cache-aside populates cached objects lazily on demand when cache misses occur.'}, {'text': 'What is Database Sharding (Horizontal Partitioning)?', 'options': ['Encrypting table columns.', 'Splitting large database tables into smaller independent chunks (shards) across multiple database server nodes based on a partition key (e.g. `user_id % num_shards`).', 'Backing up tables to tape.', 'Creating index trees.'], 'correct': 1, 'explanation': 'Sharding partitions data horizontally across database instances to overcome single-node storage and throughput limits.'}, {'text': 'What is the purpose of a Reverse Proxy / Load Balancer (e.g. NGINX, HAProxy, AWS ALB)?', 'options': ['To write JavaScript code.', 'To distribute incoming network traffic across multiple backend servers to prevent overload, handle SSL termination, and provide high availability.', 'To compile C++ code.', 'To store user passwords.'], 'correct': 1, 'explanation': 'Load balancers distribute requests evenly and mask backend server topologies from public networks.'}]},
    {'title': 'API Development', 'category': 'API Development', 'domain': 'Software Engineering', 'difficulty': 'Intermediate', 'description': 'API design principles, authentication (JWT/OAuth2), rate limiting, pagination, and error contracts.', 'time_limit_minutes': 15, 'passing_score': 70, 'icon': 'fas fa-plug', 'badge_icon': 'fas fa-award', 'questions': [{'text': 'What are the three parts of a JSON Web Token (JWT)?', 'options': ['Username, Password, and Role', 'Header (algorithm/type), Payload (claims/data), and Signature (cryptographic verification)', 'Host, Path, and Query parameters', 'Public Key, Private Key, and Salt'], 'correct': 1, 'explanation': 'JWTs consist of Base64URL-encoded Header, Payload, and Signature components separated by dots.'}, {'text': 'What is Rate Limiting in API design and why is it essential?', 'options': ['Limiting the file size of responses.', 'Restricting the number of API requests a client can make within a specified timeframe (e.g. 100 req/min) to prevent abuse, DoS attacks, and server starvation.', 'Charging credit cards per request.', 'Making APIs slower.'], 'correct': 1, 'explanation': 'Rate limiting protects service availability and defends against automated scraping and DDoS.'}, {'text': 'What is Cursor-based Pagination compared to Offset-based Pagination?', 'options': ['Offset uses `LIMIT 10 OFFSET 100`; Cursor uses a unique pointer/timestamp (e.g. `WHERE id > last_seen_id LIMIT 10`), avoiding performance degradation on deep pages.', 'Offset is faster than Cursor.', 'Cursor only works on mobile.', 'There is no difference.'], 'correct': 0, 'explanation': 'Cursor pagination leverages indexed pointers to achieve constant-time pagination across massive datasets.'}, {'text': 'What HTTP status code should an API return when a client exceeds their allocated request rate limit?', 'options': ['400 Bad Request', '429 Too Many Requests', '500 Internal Server Error', '404 Not Found'], 'correct': 1, 'explanation': '429 Too Many Requests informs clients they have exceeded allowed rate thresholds.'}, {'text': 'What is an Idempotency Key in payment and financial API transactions?', 'options': ['A secret API password.', 'A unique client-generated token passed in headers allowing servers to safely retry failed network requests without processing duplicate charges.', 'A credit card CVV.', 'A transaction database index.'], 'correct': 1, 'explanation': 'Idempotency keys prevent duplicate payments and operations when network timeouts require request retries.'}]},
    {'title': 'Unit Testing Assessment', 'category': 'Unit Testing', 'domain': 'Software Engineering', 'difficulty': 'Intermediate', 'description': 'Test-Driven Development (TDD), mocking, test isolation, coverage, and assertion libraries.', 'time_limit_minutes': 15, 'passing_score': 70, 'icon': 'fas fa-vial-circle-check', 'badge_icon': 'fas fa-award', 'questions': [{'text': 'What are the three phases of the Test-Driven Development (TDD) cycle?', 'options': ['Plan -> Code -> Deploy', 'Red (write a failing test) -> Green (write minimal code to pass) -> Refactor (clean up code while keeping tests green)', 'Build -> Test -> Release', 'Design -> Implement -> Test'], 'correct': 1, 'explanation': 'TDD follows the Red-Green-Refactor discipline to guide software construction through tests.'}, {'text': 'What is a Mock Object in unit testing?', 'options': ['A broken test.', 'A simulated object that mimics the behavior of a real external dependency (like a database or payment gateway) with pre-programmed expectations and return values to isolate the unit under test.', 'A duplicate function.', 'A test runner.'], 'correct': 1, 'explanation': 'Mocks isolate unit logic from external side effects like network and database operations.'}, {'text': "What does the 'AAA' testing pattern stand for?", 'options': ['Arrange (setup preconditions), Act (execute the behavior), Assert (verify the expected outcome)', 'Analyze, Assemble, Automate', 'Always Add Assertions', 'Authenticate, Authorize, Audit'], 'correct': 0, 'explanation': 'Arrange-Act-Assert provides a clean, predictable structure for unit test routines.'}, {'text': 'What does Code Coverage measure in test suites?', 'options': ['The number of lines of code written per day.', 'The percentage of source code lines, branches, or statements executed during automated test suite runs.', 'The execution speed of unit tests.', 'The size of the test files in KB.'], 'correct': 1, 'explanation': 'Code coverage identifies untested execution branches and code paths in the repository.'}, {'text': 'What is the primary characteristic of an effective Unit Test?', 'options': ['It tests the entire database and network simultaneously.', 'It is fast, isolated, deterministic, repeatable, and tests a single focused unit of business logic without external dependencies.', 'It runs only in production.', 'It requires human interaction.'], 'correct': 1, 'explanation': 'Good unit tests are isolated, fast, and repeatable across environments.'}]},
    {'title': 'Debugging Assessment', 'category': 'Debugging', 'domain': 'Software Engineering', 'difficulty': 'Intermediate', 'description': 'Root cause analysis, stack trace reading, breakpoints, profiling memory/CPU, and reproducing bugs.', 'time_limit_minutes': 15, 'passing_score': 70, 'icon': 'fas fa-bug', 'badge_icon': 'fas fa-award', 'questions': [{'text': 'What is a Stack Trace in software error diagnostics?', 'options': ['A list of active database queries.', 'A report showing the active execution call stack frames at the moment an unhandled exception or crash occurred, identifying exact files, functions, and line numbers.', 'A memory allocation graph.', 'A network packet dump.'], 'correct': 1, 'explanation': 'Stack traces display the call sequence leading up to an exception point.'}, {'text': 'What is Root Cause Analysis (RCA) in bug triaging?', 'options': ['Restarting the server immediately.', 'A structured problem-solving methodology aimed at identifying the fundamental underlying reason a failure occurred rather than just treating symptoms.', 'Deleting the failing code.', 'Blaming the developer who committed last.'], 'correct': 1, 'explanation': 'RCA identifies core systemic flaws to implement permanent corrective and preventive fixes.'}, {'text': 'What is the difference between a Memory Leak and High Memory Usage?', 'options': ['Memory leaks occur when allocated memory is no longer needed but never released, causing continuous growth over time; High memory usage is when a program legitimately utilizes large memory for active tasks.', 'Memory leaks only happen in Python.', 'High memory usage is always a bug.', 'There is no difference.'], 'correct': 0, 'explanation': 'Leaks reflect unbound allocation growth; high usage can be legitimate working set volume.'}, {'text': 'What does a Step-Over command do inside an interactive debugger?', 'options': ['Terminates the debugger session.', 'Executes the current line of code and pauses at the next line in the current function without diving into nested function calls.', 'Dives inside the called function.', 'Skips 10 lines.'], 'correct': 1, 'explanation': 'Step-Over executes the current statement atomically and pauses on the next sequential line.'}, {'text': 'What is a Heisenbug in software engineering?', 'options': ['A security vulnerability.', 'A software bug that seems to disappear or alter its behavior when one attempts to observe or debug it (often caused by race conditions or memory uninitialized state).', 'A bug in the Linux kernel.', 'A hardware CPU defect.'], 'correct': 1, 'explanation': 'Heisenbugs change behavior under observation due to timing and instrumentation side effects.'}]},
    {'title': 'Design Patterns Assessment', 'category': 'Design Patterns', 'domain': 'Software Engineering', 'difficulty': 'Intermediate', 'description': 'Creational (Singleton, Factory), Structural (Adapter, Decorator), and Behavioral (Observer, Strategy).', 'time_limit_minutes': 15, 'passing_score': 70, 'icon': 'fas fa-cubes', 'badge_icon': 'fas fa-award', 'questions': [{'text': 'What is the Singleton Design Pattern and what risk does it introduce?', 'options': ['A pattern that creates 100 instances.', 'A creational pattern ensuring a class has only one global instance, but which can introduce hidden dependencies and global state that complicate unit testing.', 'A pattern for sorting lists.', 'A database schema design.'], 'correct': 1, 'explanation': 'Singleton restricts instantiation to one instance while acting as a global access point.'}, {'text': 'Which behavioral design pattern defines a family of interchangeable algorithms, encapsulates each one, and allows clients to switch algorithms dynamically at runtime?', 'options': ['Strategy Pattern', 'Factory Pattern', 'Decorator Pattern', 'Singleton Pattern'], 'correct': 0, 'explanation': 'The Strategy pattern decouples algorithm implementations from client execution contexts.'}, {'text': 'What is the Observer Pattern used for in software architecture?', 'options': ['Watching video streams.', 'A subscription mechanism where multiple observer objects are automatically notified and updated whenever the subject object undergoes a state change (event-driven architecture).', 'Logging errors to disk.', 'Encrypting passwords.'], 'correct': 1, 'explanation': 'Observer establishes 1-to-many publish-subscribe dependency notifications.'}, {'text': 'What structural pattern converts the interface of an existing class into another interface expected by clients, enabling incompatible classes to work together?', 'options': ['Adapter Pattern', 'Builder Pattern', 'Composite Pattern', 'Proxy Pattern'], 'correct': 0, 'explanation': 'Adapter acts as a wrapper bridge between incompatible interfaces.'}, {'text': 'What creational pattern separates the construction of a complex object from its representation, allowing the same construction process to create various representations step-by-step?', 'options': ['Builder Pattern', 'Factory Method', 'Prototype Pattern', 'Façade Pattern'], 'correct': 0, 'explanation': 'Builder constructs complex multi-parameter objects incrementally with method chaining.'}]},
    {'title': 'Git Version Control', 'category': 'Git Version Control', 'domain': 'Software Engineering', 'difficulty': 'Beginner', 'description': 'Git workflows, branching strategies (GitFlow, Trunk-Based), hooks, and stash management.', 'time_limit_minutes': 15, 'passing_score': 70, 'icon': 'fab fa-git', 'badge_icon': 'fas fa-award', 'questions': [{'text': 'What is the difference between Trunk-Based Development and GitFlow?', 'options': ['Trunk-Based has developers merge small, frequent commits into a single `main` branch daily with feature flags; GitFlow uses multiple long-lived branches (develop, release, hotfix).', 'Trunk-Based is deprecated.', 'GitFlow does not use branches.', 'There is no difference.'], 'correct': 0, 'explanation': 'Trunk-based development emphasizes frequent mainline integration over long-lived feature branches.'}, {'text': 'What command temporarily shelves (saves) uncommitted working changes without creating a formal commit?', 'options': ['`git stash`', '`git hide`', '`git pause`', '`git hold`'], 'correct': 0, 'explanation': '`git stash` records dirty working directory state and reverts to clean HEAD.'}, {'text': 'What is a Git Hook?', 'options': ['A graphical user interface for Git.', 'Custom scripts placed in `.git/hooks` that Git executes automatically before or after events like commit (`pre-commit`), push, or receive.', 'A hardware device.', 'A remote repository URL.'], 'correct': 1, 'explanation': 'Git Hooks automate pre-commit linting, unit tests, and commit message validation locally.'}, {'text': 'What does `git commit --amend` allow you to do?', 'options': ['Delete the commit.', 'Modify the most recent commit by combining staged changes and updating the commit message.', 'Push commits to GitHub.', 'Create a new branch.'], 'correct': 1, 'explanation': '`git commit --amend` updates the tip commit snapshot and commit message.'}, {'text': 'What does `git bisect` do to assist in debugging regressions?', 'options': ['Formats code automatically.', 'Uses binary search through commit history to identify the exact commit that introduced a bug or regression.', 'Splits a branch into two.', 'Merges branches.'], 'correct': 1, 'explanation': 'Git bisect isolates defect-introducing commits efficiently via automated binary search.'}]},
    {'title': 'Agile Development', 'category': 'Agile Development', 'domain': 'Software Engineering', 'difficulty': 'Beginner', 'description': 'Scrum framework, Kanban boards, sprint ceremonies, user stories, and velocity tracking.', 'time_limit_minutes': 15, 'passing_score': 70, 'icon': 'fas fa-rotate', 'badge_icon': 'fas fa-award', 'questions': [{'text': 'In the Scrum framework, what is the role of the Product Owner?', 'options': ['Managing developer daily tasks.', 'Defining product vision, managing and prioritizing the Product Backlog, and maximizing value delivered to customers.', 'Running the server infrastructure.', 'Writing automated test cases.'], 'correct': 1, 'explanation': 'The Product Owner prioritizes backlog items based on business value and stakeholder needs.'}, {'text': 'What is the purpose of the Daily Scrum (Standup) meeting?', 'options': ['A 4-hour technical training session.', 'A 15-minute daily synchronization meeting for the development team to inspect progress toward Sprint Goals and identify blocking impediments.', 'A performance review.', 'A customer sales demo.'], 'correct': 1, 'explanation': 'Daily Standups align team momentum and surface blocking blockers.'}, {'text': 'What is a Kanban Board in Agile project management?', 'options': ['A financial spreadsheet.', 'A visual workflow management tool that displays tasks across development columns (To Do, In Progress, Review, Done) and enforces Work In Progress (WIP) limits.', 'A code compiler.', 'A time clock.'], 'correct': 1, 'explanation': 'Kanban visualizes flow and limits WIP to prevent bottlenecks.'}, {'text': 'What is Sprint Velocity in Scrum estimation?', 'options': ['The typing speed of developers.', 'The average amount of work (measured in Story Points) a development team completes during a single Sprint iteration.', 'The network download speed.', 'The server response latency.'], 'correct': 1, 'explanation': 'Velocity measures historical team throughput to forecast future sprint capacity.'}, {'text': 'What is the purpose of the Sprint Retrospective meeting?', 'options': ['To demonstrate features to the CEO.', 'A recurring meeting at the end of a sprint where the team reflects on what went well, what could be improved, and creates action items for continuous process improvement.', 'To plan next quarter budgets.', 'To review employee compensation.'], 'correct': 1, 'explanation': 'Retrospectives drive team continuous improvement and process refinements.'}]},
    {'title': 'Cybersecurity Fundamentals', 'category': 'Cybersecurity Fundamentals', 'domain': 'Cybersecurity', 'difficulty': 'Beginner', 'description': 'CIA triad, threat modeling, defense in depth, encryption fundamentals, and risk management.', 'time_limit_minutes': 15, 'passing_score': 70, 'icon': 'fas fa-shield-halved', 'badge_icon': 'fas fa-award', 'questions': [{'text': 'What are the three pillars of the CIA Triad in information security?', 'options': ['Confidentiality, Integrity, Availability', 'Control, Identification, Authentication', 'Compliance, Inspection, Auditing', 'Cryptography, Isolation, Access'], 'correct': 0, 'explanation': 'Confidentiality, Integrity, and Availability form the core foundational model for security policies.'}, {'text': 'What is the difference between Symmetric and Asymmetric Encryption?', 'options': ['Symmetric uses the same key for encryption and decryption; Asymmetric uses a mathematically paired public key and private key.', 'Symmetric is for passwords only.', 'Asymmetric does not use keys.', 'There is no difference.'], 'correct': 0, 'explanation': 'Symmetric encryption utilizes a shared single secret key; Asymmetric uses public-private keypairs.'}, {'text': 'What is Defense in Depth in security architecture?', 'options': ['Building a tall server room.', 'Employing multiple layered security controls (e.g. firewalls, MFA, encryption, intrusion detection, least privilege) throughout an IT system so that if one fails, others protect the assets.', 'Encrypting data twice with same password.', 'Disabling remote access.'], 'correct': 1, 'explanation': 'Defense in Depth deploys redundant, multi-layered security barriers.'}, {'text': 'What is Phishing in cybersecurity threats?', 'options': ['A server hardware malfunction.', 'A social engineering attack where attackers impersonate trustworthy entities via email or messaging to deceive individuals into revealing credentials or installing malware.', 'Scanning for open TCP ports.', 'A database query error.'], 'correct': 1, 'explanation': 'Phishing tricks users into disclosing confidential information via deceptive communications.'}, {'text': 'What does a Cryptographic Hash Function (e.g. SHA-256, bcrypt) guarantee?', 'options': ['Reversible encryption.', 'A one-way mathematical transformation producing a fixed-size digest that is computationally infeasible to invert or find collisions for.', 'Speeding up network connections.', 'Compressing images.'], 'correct': 1, 'explanation': 'Cryptographic hashes produce deterministic, irreversible, collision-resistant digests.'}]},
    {'title': 'Network Security', 'category': 'Network Security', 'domain': 'Cybersecurity', 'difficulty': 'Intermediate', 'description': 'Firewalls, VPNs, IDS/IPS, TLS/SSL handshakes, DNS security, and DDoS mitigation.', 'time_limit_minutes': 15, 'passing_score': 70, 'icon': 'fas fa-network-wired', 'badge_icon': 'fas fa-award', 'questions': [{'text': 'What is the purpose of TLS (Transport Layer Security) in web communications?', 'options': ['Formatting HTML pages.', 'Providing cryptographic privacy, data integrity, and server authentication over TCP networks (HTTPS) via symmetric session encryption and asymmetric handshake verification.', 'Speeding up internet bandwidth.', 'Routing IP packets.'], 'correct': 1, 'explanation': 'TLS secures communications over networks by encrypting payload data and verifying identities.'}, {'text': 'What is the difference between a Statefull Firewall and a Stateless Packet Filter?', 'options': ['Stateful firewalls track the context and state of active network connections in a state table; Stateless filters evaluate individual packets independently based on static rules.', 'Stateless firewalls are slower.', 'Stateful firewalls only block email.', 'There is no difference.'], 'correct': 0, 'explanation': 'Stateful firewalls analyze packet flow context within established connection states.'}, {'text': 'What is a Denial-of-Service (DDoS) attack and how is it mitigated?', 'options': ['A computer virus that deletes hard drives.', 'Flooding a target server with overwhelming traffic from a distributed botnet to exhaust bandwidth and compute, mitigated by CDNs, rate limiting, and traffic scrubbing.', 'Guessing passwords.', 'Stealing database records.'], 'correct': 1, 'explanation': 'DDoS overwhelms target resources with traffic volumes, mitigated by scrubbing filters and CDNs.'}, {'text': 'What security capability does a Virtual Private Network (VPN) provide to remote workers?', 'options': ['Increases home internet speed.', 'Establishes an encrypted network tunnel over public internet back to corporate networks, masking traffic and extending private subnet access securely.', 'Antivirus scanning.', 'Deletes spam emails.'], 'correct': 1, 'explanation': 'VPNs encapsulate and encrypt remote traffic across untrusted public networks.'}, {'text': 'What is a Man-in-the-Middle (MitM) attack?', 'options': ['A server rebooting during peak hours.', 'An attack where the adversary secretly intercepts and potentially alters the communications between two parties who believe they are communicating directly with each other.', 'A broken Ethernet cable.', 'An invalid SQL query.'], 'correct': 1, 'explanation': 'MitM attackers eavesdrop on or manipulate data transfers between communicating endpoints.'}]},
    {'title': 'Web Security', 'category': 'Web Security', 'domain': 'Cybersecurity', 'difficulty': 'Intermediate', 'description': 'Cross-Site Scripting (XSS), SQL Injection, CSRF, CORS policies, Content Security Policy (CSP), and secure cookies.', 'time_limit_minutes': 15, 'passing_score': 70, 'icon': 'fas fa-lock', 'badge_icon': 'fas fa-award', 'questions': [{'text': 'What is Cross-Site Scripting (XSS) and what is the primary countermeasure against it?', 'options': ['Injecting SQL commands; prevented by indexes.', 'Injecting malicious client-side JavaScript scripts into web pages viewed by other users; prevented by robust output HTML context encoding and Content Security Policy (CSP).', 'Guessing passwords; prevented by captcha.', 'Server crashes; prevented by load balancers.'], 'correct': 1, 'explanation': 'XSS executes arbitrary scripts in user browsers, mitigated by output encoding and CSP.'}, {'text': 'What is Cross-Site Request Forgery (CSRF)?', 'options': ['Stealing database tables directly.', "Tricking an authenticated user's browser into executing unwanted actions (e.g. fund transfers, password changes) on a vulnerable trusted web application via forged requests.", 'A distributed network attack.', 'A buffer overflow in C.'], 'correct': 1, 'explanation': 'CSRF exploits browser ambient credentials (cookies) to forge state-changing HTTP requests.'}, {'text': 'What security attributes should be configured on sensitive session authentication cookies?', 'options': ['`HttpOnly`, `Secure`, and `SameSite=Lax` (or `Strict`)', '`Public=true` and `Shareable=true`', '`Writable=true`', 'No attributes needed'], 'correct': 0, 'explanation': '`HttpOnly` blocks JavaScript access, `Secure` enforces HTTPS, and `SameSite` prevents CSRF.'}, {'text': 'What is the purpose of the Content-Security-Policy (CSP) HTTP response header?', 'options': ['Controls which domains the browser is allowed to load resources (scripts, images, styles, iframes) from, severely mitigating XSS attacks.', 'Sets background color of web pages.', 'Compresses CSS files.', 'Caches static assets for 1 year.'], 'correct': 0, 'explanation': 'CSP restricts unauthorized resource origins and inline script execution.'}, {'text': 'How does Cross-Origin Resource Sharing (CORS) protect users in web browsers?', 'options': ["It encrypts the user's hard drive.", 'It is a browser security mechanism using HTTP headers to permit or restrict web applications on one domain from making AJAX/fetch requests to a different origin.', 'It deletes cookies.', 'It creates database backups.'], 'correct': 1, 'explanation': 'CORS prevents unauthorized cross-origin data reads in client-side applications.'}]},
    {'title': 'OWASP Assessment', 'category': 'OWASP', 'domain': 'Cybersecurity', 'difficulty': 'Intermediate', 'description': 'OWASP Top 10 vulnerabilities (Injection, Broken Auth, SSRF, Security Misconfiguration, Insecure Design).', 'time_limit_minutes': 15, 'passing_score': 70, 'icon': 'fas fa-shield-virus', 'badge_icon': 'fas fa-award', 'questions': [{'text': 'What is Server-Side Request Forgery (SSRF) in the OWASP Top 10?', 'options': ['A client-side JavaScript bug.', 'A vulnerability where an attacker coerces a vulnerable backend server into sending unauthorized requests to internal network services or cloud metadata endpoints (e.g. `169.254.169.254`).', 'A database syntax error.', 'A phishing email.'], 'correct': 1, 'explanation': 'SSRF abuses server networking permissions to reach internal private endpoints.'}, {'text': 'What vulnerability is caused by concatenating untrusted user input directly into SQL statement strings?', 'options': ['Cross-Site Scripting (XSS)', 'SQL Injection (SQLi), remediated by using Parameterized Queries / Prepared Statements.', 'Cross-Site Request Forgery (CSRF)', 'Buffer Overflow'], 'correct': 1, 'explanation': 'SQL Injection allows adversaries to manipulate database queries via unescaped string concatenation.'}, {'text': 'What is Broken Object Level Authorization (BOLA / IDOR) in API security?', 'options': ['An application failing to verify that the requesting user has legitimate authorization to access or modify a specific resource identified by an ID parameter (e.g. `/api/orders/102`).', 'A syntax error in JSON parsing.', 'A server running out of RAM.', 'A broken database connection.'], 'correct': 0, 'explanation': 'BOLA/IDOR occurs when endpoints expose direct database keys without verifying user ownership.'}, {'text': "What does the OWASP 'Security Misconfiguration' vulnerability category encompass?", 'options': ['Writing code in Python.', 'Default passwords, unpatched systems, verbose error stack traces exposed to clients, open cloud storage buckets, and unnecessary enabled ports/services.', 'Slow internet speed.', 'Using CSS frameworks.'], 'correct': 1, 'explanation': 'Misconfigurations involve insecure defaults, open permissions, and verbose public error leaks.'}, {'text': "What does the principle of 'Secure by Default' mandate in application design?", 'options': ['Security features must be purchased as paid add-ons.', 'Software should be configured out-of-the-box with the highest security posture enabled automatically, requiring users to explicitly relax settings if needed.', 'Passwords should be stored in plain text.', 'All ports should be open by default.'], 'correct': 1, 'explanation': 'Secure by Default minimizes vulnerability exposure out-of-the-box.'}]},
    {'title': 'Ethical Hacking Assessment', 'category': 'Ethical Hacking', 'domain': 'Cybersecurity', 'difficulty': 'Advanced', 'description': 'Penetration testing methodologies, reconnaissance, port scanning (Nmap), vulnerability exploitation, and reporting.', 'time_limit_minutes': 20, 'passing_score': 70, 'icon': 'fas fa-user-secret', 'badge_icon': 'fas fa-award', 'questions': [{'text': 'What is the difference between Black Box, White Box, and Grey Box penetration testing?', 'options': ['Black Box tests with zero prior internal knowledge; White Box tests with full source code and architectural access; Grey Box tests with partial knowledge/credentials.', 'Black Box is illegal, White Box is legal.', 'White Box is automated only.', 'There is no difference.'], 'correct': 0, 'explanation': 'Testing methodologies vary based on the extent of insider knowledge and credentials provided.'}, {'text': 'What is Port Scanning in network reconnaissance?', 'options': ['Checking hardware serial numbers.', "Probing a target host's network ports using tools like Nmap to discover open services, running daemon versions, and potential attack vectors.", 'Writing SQL queries.', 'Formatting hard drives.'], 'correct': 1, 'explanation': 'Port scanning maps active listening network services and version footprints on target IPs.'}, {'text': 'What is the difference between a Vulnerability Assessment and a Penetration Test?', 'options': ['A Vulnerability Assessment identifies and catalogs known vulnerabilities; a Penetration Test actively attempts to exploit vulnerabilities to determine depth of potential compromise.', 'Vulnerability assessments are done by hackers only.', 'Pen tests do not produce reports.', 'There is no difference.'], 'correct': 0, 'explanation': 'Vulnerability assessments scan and list weaknesses; Pen tests simulate real adversary exploitation.'}, {'text': 'What is a Zero-Day Vulnerability?', 'options': ['A vulnerability that takes zero days to fix.', 'A security flaw in software that is unknown to the vendor or public, meaning zero days of patches or defenses currently exist.', 'A bug in the calendar application.', 'A free security scanner.'], 'correct': 1, 'explanation': 'Zero-day flaws have no existing manufacturer patches available at time of exploitation.'}, {'text': 'What is Privilege Escalation in ethical hacking?', 'options': ['Promoting an employee to CEO.', 'The act of exploiting a bug, design flaw, or configuration error to gain higher elevated access permissions (e.g. from low-privilege user to root/administrator).', 'Increasing network bandwidth.', 'Speeding up CPU clocks.'], 'correct': 1, 'explanation': 'Privilege escalation expands access scope from unprivileged footholds to full system control.'}]},
    {'title': 'Security Fundamentals', 'category': 'Security Fundamentals', 'domain': 'Cybersecurity', 'difficulty': 'Beginner', 'description': 'Security hygiene, password policies, multi-factor authentication (MFA), social engineering, and patch management.', 'time_limit_minutes': 15, 'passing_score': 70, 'icon': 'fas fa-shield', 'badge_icon': 'fas fa-award', 'questions': [{'text': 'What is Multi-Factor Authentication (MFA) and what three factors can it combine?', 'options': ['Using three different passwords.', 'Something you Know (password/PIN), Something you Have (authenticator app/security key), and Something you Are (biometric fingerprint/face).', 'Entering password three times.', 'Using three browsers.'], 'correct': 1, 'explanation': 'MFA combines independent authentication categories: knowledge, possession, and inherence.'}, {'text': 'Why is Password Hashing with Salt (e.g. Argon2, bcrypt) mandatory for storing user credentials?', 'options': ['It compresses passwords into 1 byte.', 'Salting appends unique random data to each password before hashing, preventing precomputed Rainbow Table attacks and ensuring identical passwords have unique stored hashes.', 'It makes passwords easy to recover.', 'It is required for HTML forms.'], 'correct': 1, 'explanation': 'Cryptographic salting defends against bulk rainbow table lookup attacks on password databases.'}, {'text': 'What is Social Engineering in information security?', 'options': ['Building social media websites.', 'Manipulating and psychologically deceiving human beings into performing actions or divulging confidential credentials and data.', 'Writing code in teams.', 'Designing office floorplans.'], 'correct': 1, 'explanation': 'Social engineering targets human psychology rather than software code vulnerabilities.'}, {'text': 'Why is regular Operating System and Software Patch Management critical for organizational security?', 'options': ['To change desktop wallpapers.', 'To remediate discovered security vulnerabilities and Common Vulnerabilities and Exposures (CVEs) before attackers exploit them.', 'To delete old user accounts.', 'To speed up mouse movement.'], 'correct': 1, 'explanation': 'Patching closes known exploitable software flaws cataloged in national CVE databases.'}, {'text': 'What is the Principle of Separation of Duties (SoD)?', 'options': ['Dividing code into separate files.', 'Requiring more than one person or system role to complete a critical or high-risk task to prevent fraud, error, and unilateral abuse of power.', 'Separating frontend from backend.', 'Using two computer monitors.'], 'correct': 1, 'explanation': 'Separation of duties disperses critical permissions across multiple authorized parties.'}]},
    {'title': 'Authentication & Authorization Assessment', 'category': 'Authentication & Authorization', 'domain': 'Cybersecurity', 'difficulty': 'Intermediate', 'description': 'OAuth 2.0 grant types, OpenID Connect (OIDC), SAML SSO, Role-Based Access Control (RBAC), and session security.', 'time_limit_minutes': 15, 'passing_score': 70, 'icon': 'fas fa-key', 'badge_icon': 'fas fa-award', 'questions': [{'text': 'What is the key difference between Authentication (AuthN) and Authorization (AuthZ)?', 'options': ['Authentication verifies WHO a user is (identity verification); Authorization determines WHAT actions and resources the authenticated user is permitted to access (permissions).', 'Authentication is for databases, Authorization is for APIs.', 'Authorization happens before Authentication.', 'There is no difference.'], 'correct': 0, 'explanation': 'AuthN establishes identity (who you are); AuthZ enforces permissions (what you can do).'}, {'text': 'In the OAuth 2.0 authorization framework, what is the role of an Access Token?', 'options': ["It is the user's plain text password.", 'A credential representing the authorization granted to a client application to access specific protected resources on behalf of the resource owner within a defined scope.', 'A database index.', 'An SSL certificate.'], 'correct': 1, 'explanation': 'Access tokens grant scoped, time-bound API access permissions to client applications.'}, {'text': 'What protocol is built as an identity layer directly on top of OAuth 2.0 to provide federated single sign-on (SSO) and ID Tokens?', 'options': ['OpenID Connect (OIDC)', 'SAML 1.0', 'Kerberos', 'FTP'], 'correct': 0, 'explanation': 'OIDC extends OAuth 2.0 with standardized JWT ID tokens containing user identity claims.'}, {'text': 'What is Role-Based Access Control (RBAC)?', 'options': ['Allowing anyone on the internet to edit tables.', 'An access control mechanism where permissions are assigned to specific functional roles (e.g. Admin, Editor, Viewer), and users acquire permissions by being assigned to those roles.', 'Encrypting passwords with salts.', 'A biometric scanner.'], 'correct': 1, 'explanation': 'RBAC organizes permissions into reusable role assignments mapped to users.'}, {'text': 'What is Session Hijacking and how is it mitigated?', 'options': ['A user logging out.', "An attacker stealing an authorized user's session token/cookie to impersonate them; mitigated by using HTTPS, HttpOnly cookies, short session timeouts, and IP/User-Agent fingerprint validation.", 'Deleting user accounts.', 'A server reboot.'], 'correct': 1, 'explanation': 'Session hijacking steals active session identifiers to impersonate legitimate users.'}]},
    {'title': 'Business Analysis', 'category': 'Business Analysis', 'domain': 'Business & Professional Skills', 'difficulty': 'Intermediate', 'description': 'SWOT analysis, process mapping, gap analysis, stakeholder engagement, and business cases.', 'time_limit_minutes': 15, 'passing_score': 70, 'icon': 'fas fa-briefcase', 'badge_icon': 'fas fa-award', 'questions': [{'text': 'What is the primary role of a Business Analyst (BA) in software and product development?', 'options': ['Writing production C++ code.', 'Bridging the gap between business stakeholders and technical engineering teams by analyzing processes, discovering requirements, and recommending data-driven solutions.', 'Managing company finances.', 'Selling software subscriptions.'], 'correct': 1, 'explanation': 'Business Analysts elicit, model, and translate business needs into actionable technical specifications.'}, {'text': 'What four quadrants comprise a standard SWOT Analysis matrix?', 'options': ['Strengths, Weaknesses, Opportunities, Threats', 'Sales, Wages, Operations, Taxes', 'Software, Web, Operating System, Testing', 'Speed, Weight, Output, Time'], 'correct': 0, 'explanation': 'SWOT assesses internal Strengths/Weaknesses and external Opportunities/Threats.'}, {'text': 'What is Gap Analysis in business consulting?', 'options': ['Calculating empty space in an office.', 'A strategic method of comparing current state performance against desired future state goals to identify missing capabilities and necessary action steps.', 'An accounting error check.', 'A database index scan.'], 'correct': 1, 'explanation': 'Gap analysis identifies deficiencies between current baseline and desired target states.'}, {'text': 'What does a RACI Matrix clarify in project stakeholder management?', 'options': ['Responsible, Accountable, Consulted, Informed', 'Requirements, Analytics, Code, Infrastructure', 'Revenue, Assets, Cash, Income', 'Risk, Action, Cost, Impact'], 'correct': 0, 'explanation': 'RACI delineates roles and accountability across operational workflow steps.'}, {'text': 'What is a Business Process Model and Notation (BPMN) diagram used for?', 'options': ['Designing database schemas.', 'Graphically depicting the end-to-end steps, decision gateways, events, and swimlanes of a business process in a standardized visual format.', 'Drawing wireframe UI designs.', 'Compiling software.'], 'correct': 1, 'explanation': 'BPMN visualizes workflow steps, decision logic, and role handoffs across organizational processes.'}]},
    {'title': 'Requirements Gathering', 'category': 'Requirements Gathering', 'domain': 'Business & Professional Skills', 'difficulty': 'Intermediate', 'description': 'Functional vs non-functional requirements, user stories, acceptance criteria, and traceability.', 'time_limit_minutes': 15, 'passing_score': 70, 'icon': 'fas fa-list-check', 'badge_icon': 'fas fa-award', 'questions': [{'text': 'What is the difference between a Functional Requirement and a Non-Functional Requirement?', 'options': ['Functional defines WHAT the system should do (features, capabilities); Non-Functional defines HOW the system should perform (speed, security, scalability, usability).', 'Functional is for developers, Non-Functional is for users.', 'Non-Functional requirements are optional.', 'There is no difference.'], 'correct': 0, 'explanation': 'Functional requirements describe specific system behaviors; Non-Functional describe quality attributes.'}, {'text': 'What is the standard format for writing Agile User Stories?', 'options': ['`As a [type of user], I want [an action/goal], so that [a benefit/value].`', '`System shall execute [code].`', '`Bug found in [file].`', '`Deploy [feature] by [date].`'], 'correct': 0, 'explanation': 'User stories encapsulate user personas, desired capabilities, and underlying value propositions.'}, {'text': 'What are Acceptance Criteria in user story specification?', 'options': ['The price of the software.', 'A predefined set of specific conditions and testable scenarios that a software deliverable must satisfy before the user story is marked as complete and accepted by stakeholders.', 'The programming language chosen.', "The developer's hourly wage."], 'correct': 1, 'explanation': 'Acceptance criteria define precise boundaries for when a feature is considered fully done.'}, {'text': 'What is Scope Creep in project requirements management?', 'options': ['Writing clean code.', 'The uncontrolled, continuous expansion of project requirements or features beyond the original agreed baseline without corresponding adjustments to budget, time, or resources.', 'Deleting old requirements.', 'A database backup.'], 'correct': 1, 'explanation': 'Scope creep introduces delays and budget overruns through unmanaged requirement inflation.'}, {'text': 'What is a Requirements Traceability Matrix (RTM)?', 'options': ['A document that tracks user passwords.', 'A grid linking business requirements from their origin through design specifications, code implementation, and testing test cases to ensure complete validation coverage.', 'A bug tracking spreadsheet.', 'A financial audit report.'], 'correct': 1, 'explanation': 'RTM verifies that every specified requirement is validated by corresponding test suites.'}]},
    {'title': 'Product Management', 'category': 'Product Management', 'domain': 'Business & Professional Skills', 'difficulty': 'Intermediate', 'description': 'Product roadmaps, OKRs, customer empathy, feature prioritization (MoSCoW, RICE), and metrics.', 'time_limit_minutes': 15, 'passing_score': 70, 'icon': 'fas fa-lightbulb', 'badge_icon': 'fas fa-award', 'questions': [{'text': 'What does the RICE feature prioritization scoring framework stand for?', 'options': ['Reach, Impact, Confidence, Effort', 'Revenue, Innovation, Cost, Execution', 'Risk, Infrastructure, Code, Evaluation', 'Roadmap, Iteration, Customer, Experience'], 'correct': 0, 'explanation': 'RICE scores proposed features via `(Reach * Impact * Confidence) / Effort`.'}, {'text': 'What is a Minimum Viable Product (MVP)?', 'options': ['The final completed version of a product.', 'A version of a new product with just enough core features to be usable by early customers and gather validated learning with the least effort.', 'A product with zero bugs.', 'A prototype built on paper.'], 'correct': 1, 'explanation': 'MVPs validate core value hypotheses rapidly with real user feedback.'}, {'text': 'What is the difference between OKRs (Objectives and Key Results) and KPIs?', 'options': ['Objectives define what you want to achieve (ambitious qualitative goals) with quantifiable Key Results; KPIs are ongoing operational metrics measuring business health.', 'OKRs are for engineering only, KPIs are for sales.', 'KPIs are replaced by OKRs.', 'There is no difference.'], 'correct': 0, 'explanation': 'OKRs drive strategic change and growth targets; KPIs monitor operational baseline health.'}, {'text': 'What does Product-Market Fit (PMF) signify for a startup product?', 'options': ['Securing venture capital funding.', "The stage where a company's product successfully satisfies a strong market demand and customers are actively using, paying for, and retaining on the product.", 'Filing a patent.', 'Hiring 100 employees.'], 'correct': 1, 'explanation': "PMF represents strong alignment between a product's value proposition and market demand."}, {'text': 'What is a Product Roadmap?', 'options': ['A GPS navigation map.', 'A strategic, high-level visual communication tool that outlines the vision, priorities, major milestones, and evolution of a product over time.', 'A list of bug tickets in Jira.', 'A software architecture diagram.'], 'correct': 1, 'explanation': 'Product roadmaps communicate strategic trajectory and thematic development milestones to stakeholders.'}]},
    {'title': 'Project Management', 'category': 'Project Management', 'domain': 'Business & Professional Skills', 'difficulty': 'Intermediate', 'description': 'Critical Path Method (CPM), Gantt charts, risk registers, resource allocation, and triple constraint.', 'time_limit_minutes': 15, 'passing_score': 70, 'icon': 'fas fa-diagram-project', 'badge_icon': 'fas fa-award', 'questions': [{'text': 'What are the three competing variables comprising the Project Management Triple Constraint (Iron Triangle)?', 'options': ['Scope, Time (Schedule), and Cost (Budget) (all constraining Quality)', 'Code, Test, and Deploy', 'People, Process, and Technology', 'Sales, Marketing, and Operations'], 'correct': 0, 'explanation': 'The Triple Constraint governs that changes in Scope, Schedule, or Cost inevitably impact Quality.'}, {'text': 'What is the Critical Path in the Critical Path Method (CPM) project scheduling?', 'options': ['The path with the most expensive tasks.', 'The longest continuous sequence of dependent project activities that determines the shortest possible total duration required to complete the project (zero float/slack).', 'The easiest tasks in the project.', 'The emergency exit route.'], 'correct': 1, 'explanation': 'Critical path activities have zero float; any delay directly postpones project completion.'}, {'text': 'What is a Work Breakdown Structure (WBS)?', 'options': ['A computer crashing under load.', 'A hierarchical decomposition of the total project scope into smaller, more manageable deliverables and work packages.', 'A project budget spreadsheet.', 'A list of project risks.'], 'correct': 1, 'explanation': 'WBS decomposes project deliverables into distinct, manageable work elements.'}, {'text': 'What is a Risk Register (Risk Matrix) used for in project governance?', 'options': ['Logging employee sick days.', 'A centralized document that identifies potential project risks, assesses their probability and impact, and defines mitigation action plans and risk owners.', 'Recording software bugs.', 'Tracking invoice payments.'], 'correct': 1, 'explanation': 'Risk registers monitor potential hazards and document proactive mitigation strategies.'}, {'text': 'What does a Gantt Chart visually display in project schedules?', 'options': ['Financial cash flow.', 'A horizontal bar chart illustrating a project schedule, displaying start/end dates, durations, and dependency linkages across project tasks over a chronological timeline.', 'Team organizational chart.', 'Server network topologies.'], 'correct': 1, 'explanation': 'Gantt charts map task timelines, dependencies, and milestones across calendar schedules.'}]},
    {'title': 'Communication', 'category': 'Communication', 'domain': 'Business & Professional Skills', 'difficulty': 'Beginner', 'description': 'Active listening, constructive feedback, empathy, non-verbal cues, and cross-functional alignment.', 'time_limit_minutes': 15, 'passing_score': 70, 'icon': 'fas fa-comments', 'badge_icon': 'fas fa-award', 'questions': [{'text': 'What is Active Listening in professional communication?', 'options': ['Interrupting immediately to give advice.', 'Fully concentrating, understanding, responding, and remembering what is being said rather than just passively hearing the speaker.', 'Recording conversations secretly.', 'Listening to background music while typing.'], 'correct': 1, 'explanation': 'Active listening involves focused engagement, paraphrasing for understanding, and thoughtful responses.'}, {'text': 'What is the SBI (Situation-Behavior-Impact) model for delivering constructive feedback?', 'options': ['Specific, Brief, Immediate', 'Describing the specific Situation, outlining the observable Behavior, and explaining the tangible Impact it had.', 'Start, Build, Iterate', 'Standard, Business, Internal'], 'correct': 1, 'explanation': 'The SBI framework provides objective, non-judgmental feedback grounded in observed impact.'}, {'text': 'Why is psychological safety important for high-performing technical teams?', 'options': ['It guarantees zero mistakes ever happen.', 'It creates a team climate where members feel safe to take interpersonal risks, ask questions, voice dissenting opinions, and admit errors without fear of punishment.', 'It eliminates all deadlines.', 'It prevents disagreement.'], 'correct': 1, 'explanation': 'Psychological safety fosters innovation, transparent problem reporting, and candid collaboration.'}, {'text': "What is the '7 Cs of Effective Communication' framework?", 'options': ['Clear, Concise, Concrete, Correct, Coherent, Complete, Courteous', 'Code, Compile, Commit, Cloud, Continuous, Complete, Check', 'Fast, Friendly, Financial, Focused, Formal, Frank, Flexible', 'Creative, Clever, Critical, Complex, Conceptual, Constant, Calm'], 'correct': 0, 'explanation': 'The 7 Cs provide guidelines for ensuring clear, unambiguous verbal and written messages.'}, {'text': 'What does Non-Verbal Communication include in face-to-face or video meetings?', 'options': ['The text written in the chat box.', 'Body language, facial expressions, eye contact, posture, tone of voice, and physical gestures.', 'Email attachments.', 'Slides in PowerPoint.'], 'correct': 1, 'explanation': 'Non-verbal cues convey emotional context and reinforce spoken messaging.'}]},
    {'title': 'Problem Solving', 'category': 'Problem Solving', 'domain': 'Business & Professional Skills', 'difficulty': 'Intermediate', 'description': 'Root cause analysis (5 Whys), first principles thinking, decision trees, and brainstorming.', 'time_limit_minutes': 15, 'passing_score': 70, 'icon': 'fas fa-puzzle-piece', 'badge_icon': 'fas fa-award', 'questions': [{'text': "What is the '5 Whys' technique used for in problem diagnosis?", 'options': ['Asking five random questions.', "Iteratively asking 'Why?' five times to peel away symptom layers and drill down to the foundational root cause of a defect or problem.", 'Interviewing five witnesses.', 'Writing five unit tests.'], 'correct': 1, 'explanation': 'The 5 Whys methodology surfaces underlying systemic root causes rather than superficial symptoms.'}, {'text': 'What is First Principles Thinking (Reasoning from First Principles)?', 'options': ['Reasoning by copying what competitors are doing.', 'Breaking a complex problem down to its most fundamental, indisputable truths and building up a solution from scratch without assumptions.', 'Guessing the answer.', 'Using the first search result on Google.'], 'correct': 1, 'explanation': 'First principles thinking deconstructs problems into fundamental physics/truths to innovate.'}, {'text': 'What is the Pareto Principle (80/20 Rule) in problem resolution and prioritization?', 'options': ['All problems require equal time to solve.', 'Roughly 80% of consequences or defects come from 20% of the underlying causes, meaning focusing on vital few root causes yields the largest impact.', '80% of workers do no work.', '20% of code has no bugs.'], 'correct': 1, 'explanation': 'The 80/20 rule guides focusing resources on the critical few drivers that cause the majority of issues.'}, {'text': 'What is Divergent Thinking vs Convergent Thinking in creative problem solving?', 'options': ['Divergent generates a broad variety of creative possibilities and ideas; Convergent evaluates and narrows options down to the single best optimal solution.', 'Divergent is slow, Convergent is fast.', 'Divergent is for math only.', 'There is no difference.'], 'correct': 0, 'explanation': 'Divergent thinking expands exploratory possibilities; convergent thinking filters and optimizes.'}, {'text': 'What is a Fishbone Diagram (Ishikawa Diagram) used for?', 'options': ['Designing seafood restaurant menus.', 'A visual cause-and-effect tool that categorizes potential causes of a problem (People, Process, Machine, Material, Environment) to find root causes.', 'Plotting database schemas.', 'Drawing network paths.'], 'correct': 1, 'explanation': 'Fishbone diagrams systematically map multi-factorial causes of operational defects.'}]},
